{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the angel of HaShem appeared to him in a flame of fire from the midst of the bush.</b> Regarding this vision there are many opinions, for some say it alludes to ״עִמּוֹ אָנֹכִי בְצָרָה״ ('I am with him in distress'); therefore He appeared to him in the bush — a lowly and humble tree. And according to Rashi's explanation it is not at all settled why specifically in the bush. And some say that the bush alludes to the sufferings of Israel, for the fire of troubles blazes around it yet it is not consumed, after the manner of ״חִצַּי כָּלִים וְהֵם אֵינָן כָּלִים״. And in Bereshit Rabbah many similes are also found. And according to what I explained above on the verse ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 1:12), that the Egyptians were likened to thorns, for they were to Israel a painful thorn, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, showed him that the bush is not consumed — for it alludes to Pharaoh: even though the fire of troubles blazes around him — wrath and fury and affliction — nevertheless he is not consumed, for the Holy One, blessed be He, gives him strength to endure all the plagues, as it is written (Exodus 9:16) ״וְאוּלָם בַּעֲבוּר זֹאת הֶעֱמַדְתִּיךָ בַּעֲבוּר הַרְאֹתְךָ אֶת כֹּחִי״." "Another matter: it alluded to what is said, ״אָכֵן נוֹדַע הַדָּבָר״ — for what reason are Israel immersed in distress more than all the nations? Because among them there are informants who raise a voice of outcry with insults and reviled language against both sleeping and waking (Sanhedrin 29a) — like these thorns that produce a sound when one kindles them in fire. So too Israel: even at a time when the fire of troubles blazes around them, nevertheless each one is to his fellow a painful thorn and a searing pot, after the manner of these generations, and they constantly raise a voice like the crackling of thorns under a pot, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:6) ״כִּי כְקוֹל הַסִּירִים תַּחַת הַסִּיר כֵן שְׂחוֹק הַכְּסִיל״. And this is the principal cause for Israel to be in exile — on account of the hatred and envy among them, more than among all the nations. And the slanderers have made all of Israel non-clean (נָקִי), for the fire of strife goes out from the rock of strife: the fathers kindle the fire and the sons gather wood; therefore the fire of HaShem blazed against them. Therefore it is said ״וְהַסְּנֶה אֵינֶנּוּ אֻכָּל״, for even though the fire of afflictions blazes in Jacob, nevertheless the existence of the bush endures in every generation and is not consumed, for the thorns cannot consume the vineyard of HaShem of Hosts, the house of Israel." "<b>And with this it is settled what is said, 'in a flame of fire from the midst of the bush.'</b> For it should have said ״בְּלַבַּת אֵשׁ בַּסְּנֶה״ or ״בְּתוֹךְ הַסְּנֶה״ — what is ״מִתּוֹךְ הַסְּנֶה״? And likewise what is said, ״וְהִנֵּה הַסְּנֶה בֹּעֵר בָּאֵשׁ״ — it should be the reverse: ״וְהִנֵּה הָאֵשׁ בּוֹעֵר בַּסְּנֶה״, for the fire is the agent and the bush is the patient. But the truth is as we have said: the bush kindles the fire of troubles, for since Israel is likened to this bush — that each one is to his fellow a painful thorn, raising his voice against him with insults and reviled language — therefore the fire of HaShem blazed against them. And if so, the bush is the agent and the fire is the patient; therefore it is said ״וְהִנֵּה הַסְּנֶה בֹּעֵר בָּאֵשׁ״, for the bush causes the kindling of the fire. And this is what it says: ״בְּלַבַּת אֵשׁ מִתּוֹךְ הַסְּנֶה״ — for the flame of fire is drawn from the midst of the bush, for the bush causes the flame of fire. And this explanation is correct."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "I read וְסִלּוֹן מַמְאִיר as a variant spelling of סִיר ('pot'), intensifying the root מ־א־ר (to sear/scorch), completing the image from Ecclesiastes 7:6 that the Kli Yakar cites next."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:13.684609316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}